{% extends "base.njk" %}
{% block main %}
      <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[800px] flex-1 px-gutter py-12">
        <p class="text-micro text-muted pb-3">Privacy</p>
        <h1 class="text-h2 font-bold tracking-h2 text-balance pb-3">
          Your photos and check-ins stay on your device
        </h1>
        <p class="text-micro text-muted pb-8">
          Effective date: September 14, 2026. Last updated: 2026-10-01
        </p>

        <div class="[&>*+*]:mt-[1em] [&_h2]:mt-[2.25em] [&_h2]:text-h3 [&_h2]:leading-h3 [&_h2]:tracking-h3 [&_h2]:font-bold [&_h3]:mt-[1.75em] [&_h3]:text-[1.0625rem] [&_h3]:leading-[1.35] [&_h3]:tracking-h4 [&_h3]:font-bold [&_p]:text-muted [&_li]:text-muted [&_strong]:text-fg [&_strong]:font-semibold [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_ol]:list-decimal [&_ol]:pl-5">
          <p>
            Recomp is a local-first iOS app. Recomp never uploads your photos or
            weight, or the notes and other details you add to a check-in. It
            does not create an account for you and does not store your physique
            data on a Recomp server. It does send anonymous usage analytics and
            crash reports, with no tracking, and the section below lists what
            they contain.
          </p>

          <h2>What the app stores</h2>
          <p>
            Check-ins live in the app on your iPhone. Photos you import come
            from your Photos library; Recomp does not scan your camera roll in
            the background or send those images anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you back up your phone with iCloud or a computer, those backups
            follow Apple’s rules — the same as the rest of your photos. That is
            your backup, not a Recomp upload.
          </p>

          <h2>What we do not do</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>No photo upload to Recomp</li>
            <li>No social feed or sharing built into the app</li>
            <li>No AI body-fat estimates</li>
            <li>No advertising SDKs in the app for selling your data</li>
            <li>No tracking you across other apps or websites from the app</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Anonymous usage analytics</h2>
          <p>
            The app can send anonymous usage analytics through PostHog: basic
            events such as a check-in created or edited, the compare screen
            opened, or a permission prompt answered, with simple counts and
            categories, plus device and app info (model, iOS version, app
            version, locale) and a random ID for that install. It does not use
            an account, identify you, or track you across apps (no IDFA), and
            it never sends photos, weight values, dates, measurements, notes,
            or any other text. Analytics include approximate location (city,
            region, and country) derived from your IP address, used only for
            anonymous analytics and not linked to your identity. The IP address
            itself is not stored. The app also sends anonymous error reports
            when something breaks: the error type and where in the app's code
            it happened, plus the same device and app info and random install
            ID, with messages trimmed so they do not include notes, photo
            names, or file paths.
          </p>

          <h2>This website</h2>
          <p>
            This website is a marketing site. It does not receive your
            in-app photos. It uses PostHog to record visits and clicks on these
            pages — not the contents of the app.
          </p>

          <h2>Contact</h2>
          <p>
            Questions:
            <a href="mailto:support@progressphotos.app">support@progressphotos.app</a>
          </p>
        </div>
